  "writing_style": "Voice: neutral and authoritative. Suitable for compliance or regulated contexts.\nVocabulary: complete, proper terms. No slang, no casual asides, no contractions (\"do not\", not \"don't\").\nSentence structure: complete, well-formed sentences — but concise; formal does not mean verbose.\nPoint of view: address the reader as \"you\" or use neutral phrasing (\"Users may…\"); maintain a professional distance.\nDo: \"Select **Save** to apply your changes.\" / \"Ensure the certificate is valid before proceeding.\"\nDon't: \"Just hit save and you're good to go.\" / emoji, slang, or jokey asides.\n\nAudience — end users/customers: assume no technical background. Avoid jargon entirely or define it in plain terms. Focus on the task they want to complete, not the mechanism behind it.\n\nMaturity — mature/enterprise: thorough and dependable in tone. Readers expect stability, completeness, and precision.",
  "project_name": "Arto Manual",
  "article_title": "Write and publish an article",
  "article_description": "English, formal and simple. Audience: editors at Arto (hopp-wordpress) publications. Covers creating a new post in the WordPress block editor, using Arto's custom blocks (lead/ingress, fact box, image, article summary, article abstract, table, teaser, teaser group, listicle heading, podcast, form, map position, respondent, service teaser, signers list — see web/app/themes/hopp/editor/blocks), saving drafts, previewing (including sharing a preview of an unpublished post via the Public Post Preview plugin), scheduling, publishing, duplicating a post (classes/Duplicate_Post.php) and setting an automatic expiry date (inc/post-expirator.php). Source code: hopp-wordpress folder in the workspace (read-only).",
  "article_type": "how-to"
}
